From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:31:35 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: xrx500-cbm: let the tagging driver own the switch header

The conduit built the eight-byte switch header itself and sent every
frame out one port fixed at probe. With the switch ports being network
devices of their own, the tagging driver writes that header and records
the port it chose in the socket buffer, so the conduit's job on transmit
is to copy the frame exactly as it was handed over and to send it
through the egress resources of the port named for it.

Those resources are separate per port - a dequeue port, a
traffic-management queue and a DMA channel each - so the conduit is
allocated one transmit queue per port it serves and decodes the port out
of the queue mapping. The queue is load bearing rather than decorative:
the core skips the selection callback entirely on a device with a single
transmit queue and then overwrites the mapping with zero, which would
send every frame out the first port with no error anywhere. Queue zero
is reserved for a frame that names no port, which is any frame from the
local stack; such a frame has no switch header in front of it, the
switch would read its first eight bytes as one, and there is nothing
sensible to do with it but count it and drop it.

Back pressure now stops and releases every queue rather than the first,
which on a multi-queue device is not the same thing.

On receive the header stays in front of the frame. The helper that
classifies an Ethernet frame consumes the header together with the first
six bytes behind it, which is exactly the offset the tagging driver
expects to find the header at, and on a device serving a switch the same
helper answers with the protocol that routes the frame there. The source
port is still decoded, but only to be counted.

The conduit's maximum transmission unit grows by the header, because the
switch layer sizes the conduit from a port's own ceiling plus the
tagging overhead and refuses to bring a port up if the conduit cannot
carry it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

